{
	title: 'The event loop never blocks over 10 ms',
	spec: `Hal never freezes. No piece of work on the host or on a client (terminal or web) holds the event loop for more than 10 ms: work that can grow with history size or tab count (laying out and painting history, writing a big frame to the terminal, decoding history pages, building marks indexes, replaying a session, loading tabs, reading or writing files on a hot path) runs asynchronously or in slices that yield to the scheduler between them, and nothing spins a core. Keys, frames and host events keep flowing while such work runs; it may finish later, never block.

The rule is measured, not assumed: perf.watch (common/perf.ts) runs a timer every 5 ms and reports each time it fires more than 10 ms late. main starts it when HAL_STALLS names a file and appends one line per block, pid, wall-clock ms and length ms. scripts/perf (task te) sets HAL_STALLS for every process it launches, prints per setup (host cold and warm, peer, -r, tab switching, web page load) the count, longest and total of blocks over 10 ms with the worst ones and when they happened, and fails if any block exceeds 10 ms. Garbage-collection pauses cannot be told apart and count too: holding less memory is how they are kept short. Histories are streamed line by line (asonl); single ASON files stay small (about 1 MB at most), so parsing one is not sliced.`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		"Baseline before any fix (generated home scale 1, load ~2): blocks over 10 ms per setup, count/longest/total: host cold 11/674/1457 ms, host warm 17/518/981, peer 8/755/1037, -r 16/629/1153, tab switching 23/111/497, web page load (host) 3/16/40. The long ones come about 2-3 s after start: the focused 50 MB tab's backfill drawn into scrollback in one go.",
		'Terminal client slicing (frame.layout deadline, render past/tail, terminal.writer async output, backfill.apply without stringifying all pages). scripts/perf on the same generated home, before -> after, count/longest/total ms: host cold 7/672/1413 -> 8/668/814, host warm 9/511/817 -> 9/73/244, peer 23/515/1238 -> 8/51/148, -r 9/574/796 -> 3/49/86, tab switching 33/1120/1837 -> 7/39/119, web page load 1/526/526 -> 3/62/102. Key p99 went from 558-679 ms to 77-136 ms; what remains of it is key frames queued behind a multi-megabyte repaint the pty reader consumes slowly. What is left over 10 ms is host-side (startup, pages/marks answering in-process: host cold 668 ms at 0.7 s is the host) plus about 10-12 ms per key in the in-process host draft path (drafts.flush -> host act -> broadcast/save). Profile with HAL_STALLS set, not by grouping CPU-profile samples: sliced work with 1 ms gaps looks like one long run in the profile.',
		"Host side (pages.ts steps + slices, host holds a client's commands for a session while its open is read, tabs' states computed in slices before the tabs event, wire.ts skips copying history records, status caches derived states). scripts/perf, count/longest/total ms, after the terminal slicing (fe1f1c7) -> after this: host cold 8/668/814 -> 7/33/119, host warm 9/73/244 -> 4/46/113, peer 8/51/148 -> 6/17/75, -r 3/49/86 -> 4/18/54, tab switching 7/39/119 -> 1/13/13, web page load 3/62/102 -> 5/22/70. ASON parsing itself is the cost of decoding (ason.ts is verbatim, task mw): a 1 MB string field takes ~5 ms (an intermediate host-only run on 18076d7 cut host cold from 10/676/1506 with the terminal repaint still whole). Left: the host warm 46 ms at 0.1 s and 20-35 ms at startup are process start (module load, config/liveFile reads, first frame) plus GC; a single record of several MB still costs its own decode (~5-10 ms per 2 MB) since one record is never split; the socket/WebSocket path still stringifies a page in one go (adapt: ~8-20 ms for a 4 MB page); ~10 ms per key in the in-process draft path is untouched.",
		'Key-output investigation at 61e7449 (scale 1 generated home): two clean runs p99/max ms, host cold 82/170 and 85/113; host warm 206/206 and 335/335; peer 223/223 and 61/61; -r 227/227 and 111/111. Cold longest stalls were 835 and 342 ms. CPU profiling reproduced 415 ms in turnRecovery.recover -> leftWork -> pages.marks -> drive(catchUp): the 1 s first-frame fallback starts recovery before sliced tab indexing finishes. This race also exists at 3652846; that revision ran cold longest 33 ms when indexing beat the fallback. Recovery now awaits sliced marks catch-up first. After this change on 61e7449: cold p99/max 52/108 ms, warm 88/88, peer 119/119, -r 126/126; tab-switch max 24 ms; cold longest stall 46 ms. A one-time forced-immediate startup fallback probe gave key p99/max 37/37 and longest stall 36 ms. Full test passed 1267 tests, tsc and lint. An async local transport experiment did not improve measured key latency and was removed. The remaining key latency is still ordered terminal-output backlog, synchronous draft persistence and startup/GC/catalog work; no claim that the 50 ms key or 10 ms event-loop goals are met.',
		'Safe repaint preemption is not a writer queue-priority change: paintParts advances prev/cursorRow to the completed future frame, and SYNC_ON spans the whole repaint. Chunks can end inside escape sequences, UTF-8 or soft-wrapped URL chains. A future progressive repaint must expose complete screen-consistent checkpoints with known physical cursor/style/link/sync state, keep a live prompt/footer independent of history scrollback, and compute later diffs from actually committed screen state. Cap bytes in flight and test final screen, scrollback and link/soft-wrap equality against an uninterrupted canonical repaint. Existing writer already waits for each 64 KiB async write callback; smaller FIFO pieces alone cannot make the prompt bypass megabytes. Quit/suspend currently synchronously flush queued repaint output but cannot wait for the write already in flight: restoration/park bytes may overtake or interleave that last chunk. A slow terminal may delay quitting; quitting during repaint can leave partial/stale rows, wrong cursor or an incomplete escape/mode sequence until the shell resets it. Suspend can show the same issue until resume forces a redraw. Frequency depends on quitting during a large slow repaint; no measured rate. Recovery slicing does not alter terminal output or this risk.'
	],
	needs: ['te']
}
